  "tour": {
    "title": "The Divine Council: From the Old Testament to Jesus and Paul",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the basic vocabulary and picture: Yahweh as supreme King working with a heavenly staff. It then follows the Old Testament’s Babel, Deuteronomy 32, and Psalm 82 assumptions before showing how Jesus and Paul read that world and announce its reversal. The teachers are not perfectly uniform, so one clip also names an interpretive disagreement instead of hiding it.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e3-spiritual-warfare",
        "startSec": 0,
        "endSec": 180,
        "why": "Begin here for the simplest orientation. The speaker explains that Elohim can name a broad class of spiritual beings while Yahweh is uniquely supreme, then defines the council as God’s “staff team, with delegated authority.” It also tells you why this matters: Jesus and Paul use the vocabulary of “spiritual powers, rulers, authorities.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Spiritual Warfare",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387",
        "durationSec": 4363,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e3-spiritual-warfare",
        "startSec": 360,
        "endSec": 900,
        "why": "This puts flesh on the definition through 1 Kings 22, Job, and the biblical titles “host of heaven” and “sons of Elohim.” Listen for the explanation that host means an army and for the clarification that “sons of Elohim” means members of a class, not necessarily biological offspring. The council becomes a scene of real delegated agency rather than a vague picture of angels.",
        "episodeTitle": "Spiritual Warfare",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=b40c2f6a-4f0a-4552-a28b-ad3991133387",
        "durationSec": 4363,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e8-god-series-live-qr",
        "startSec": 1930,
        "endSec": 2110,
        "why": "Now follow the Old Testament’s national framework. The speaker reads Deuteronomy 32 as recalling the Most High giving the nations their inheritance and fixing their boundaries “according to the number of the sons of Elohim.” The resulting claim is that Babel is not merely language confusion: God was “disinheriting humanity,” while choosing Israel as the line through which he would bring a new humanity.",
        "episodeTitle": "God Question & Response 2 - Live!",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/15175b04-afcd-4819-aabd-3a474ce1c9cd/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=15175b04-afcd-4819-aabd-3a474ce1c9cd",
        "durationSec": 2833,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:theme-god-e4-origin-spiritual-beings-mini-qr",
        "startSec": 2500,
        "endSec": 3200,
        "why": "This is the key Old Testament case study. It connects the Genesis 6 rebellion to Psalm 82, where God stands “in the council of El” and asks, “How long will you judge unjustly?” The clip explains how the speakers associate heavenly rulers with earthly injustice and delegated authority, ending with God’s judgment that these rulers’ “days are numbered.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Origin of Spiritual Beings + Mini Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=d7e45e15-6a73-4ddc-a3a7-42e0547ef789",
        "durationSec": 4082,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:ee2d4ff5-com-6e99511a-003e-300d-be0a-42d127b215f3",
        "startSec": 650,
        "endSec": 830,
        "why": "Here you hear Jesus’ use of Psalm 82 in John 10. The commentator lays out both major readings—that the psalm addresses human rulers or supernatural beings—and says the divine-assembly reading “probably” fits the psalm’s scene. That makes the New Testament connection concrete without pretending every interpreter agrees.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 10:22-42",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/y9bntfdedmsazpzr/john_1022-42929g5.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1399,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:225",
        "startSec": 133,
        "endSec": 440,
        "why": "This clip shows the worldview moving into Acts. It explains the link among Genesis 10–11, Deuteronomy 32, the nations’ subjection to lesser divine beings, and Pentecost as the beginning of their reclamation. The phrase to listen for is “the reversal of that, this whole situation, reclaiming the nations,” which gives the council theme a mission-shaped New Testament payoff.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 37: Acts 2:1-21",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/02/NakedBible37_Acts2_1_21.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3857,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:2105",
        "startSec": 2430,
        "endSec": 2580,
        "why": "Land on Paul’s interpretation of Christ’s victory in Colossians 2. The speaker distinguishes cancellation of the human “record of debt” from the rulers’ being “disarmed and put to shame,” then carefully says the powers are not destroyed yet but “displaced or removed from the authority they held over the Gentiles.” That is the tour’s final lens for reading New Testament claims about the cross, resurrection, and spiritual powers.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 232: Colossians 2:13-23",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/NakedBible232_Col2_13_23.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3973,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You should now be able to recognize the council as a recurring biblical picture: Yahweh alone is Creator and sovereign, yet he governs through heavenly beings with delegated authority; rebellion corrupts that arrangement and becomes entangled with earthly injustice and the nations. In the New Testament, Jesus’ identity, the mission to the nations, and Paul’s language about rulers and authorities all become sharper when read against that Old Testament backdrop.",
    "totalSeconds": 2237
  },
